WEBVTT

00:00:00.321 --> 00:00:03.065
Last video ended with saving instead of borrowing:

00:00:03.546 --> 00:00:05.289
two hundred dollars into an account at the

00:00:05.329 --> 00:00:07.593
end of every month, towards a first apartment,

00:00:08.115 --> 00:00:09.336
and the balance climbs.

00:00:10.138 --> 00:00:11.786
Now put a second account beside it.

00:00:12.389 --> 00:00:14.189
A hundred thousand dollars sits in a fund,

00:00:14.731 --> 00:00:17.492
and one thousand and fifty dollars ninety-one comes

00:00:17.753 --> 00:00:18.952
out at the end of every month,

00:00:19.454 --> 00:00:20.634
so the balance falls.

00:00:21.537 --> 00:00:23.832
One grows and one shrinks, but they're the

00:00:23.873 --> 00:00:24.738
same kind of thing.

00:00:25.565 --> 00:00:26.857
What do these two have in common?

00:00:27.387 --> 00:00:29.631
An annuity is a run of equal payments

00:00:29.752 --> 00:00:32.797
made at regular times, with interest compounding on

00:00:32.838 --> 00:00:34.099
the balance every period.

00:00:34.742 --> 00:00:36.970
Compounding means the interest is added to the

00:00:36.991 --> 00:00:39.902
balance, so next period it earns interest too.

00:00:40.647 --> 00:00:41.532
There are two kinds.

00:00:41.996 --> 00:00:44.424
In the first, you make equal contributions into

00:00:44.445 --> 00:00:46.753
an investment account, and interest is added at

00:00:46.794 --> 00:00:47.957
the end of each period.

00:00:48.661 --> 00:00:51.251
In the second, you invest one single sum,

00:00:51.554 --> 00:00:53.401
then make equal withdrawals from it.

00:00:53.965 --> 00:00:56.540
Same size, same gap, the same rate each

00:00:56.622 --> 00:00:57.003
period.

00:00:57.509 --> 00:00:58.601
That's the pattern to look for.

00:00:59.297 --> 00:01:00.206
Let's test the idea.

00:01:01.015 --> 00:01:03.712
Two thousand dollars invested once and left to

00:01:03.753 --> 00:01:03.973
grow.

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:05.525
Is that an annuity?

00:01:06.175 --> 00:01:06.622
Make a guess.

00:01:07.699 --> 00:01:08.002
It isn't.

00:01:08.509 --> 00:01:10.652
It earns compound interest, but there are no

00:01:10.774 --> 00:01:13.598
regular payments, so it's just compound interest, like

00:01:13.619 --> 00:01:15.582
the growth models in the sequences videos.

00:01:16.506 --> 00:01:19.214
Deposits of fifty, then eighty, then a hundred

00:01:19.235 --> 00:01:20.096
and ten dollars a month

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:22.906
aren't equal, so that's not an annuity either.

00:01:23.428 --> 00:01:25.294
And putting money in whenever you have spare

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:26.498
cash isn't regular.

00:01:27.241 --> 00:01:28.945
But two hundred dollars at the end of

00:01:29.166 --> 00:01:31.652
every quarter, with interest each quarter, is.

00:01:32.362 --> 00:01:34.399
Now look inside the apartment savings.

00:01:35.143 --> 00:01:36.789
Say it earns six per cent a year,

00:01:36.989 --> 00:01:39.739
compounded monthly, which is zero point five per

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:40.402
cent a month.

00:01:41.104 --> 00:01:43.488
So each month the balance is multiplied by

00:01:43.669 --> 00:01:45.131
one point zero zero five,

00:01:45.633 --> 00:01:47.275
and then the two hundred dollar deposit goes

00:01:47.336 --> 00:01:47.395
in.

00:01:48.318 --> 00:01:50.363
Month one starts with nothing, so there's no

00:01:50.403 --> 00:01:52.648
interest, and the balance is just two hundred.

00:01:53.611 --> 00:01:55.955
In month two, the two hundred earns one

00:01:56.016 --> 00:01:58.800
dollar, then the next deposit arrives, giving four

00:01:58.841 --> 00:01:59.621
hundred and one dollars.

00:02:00.645 --> 00:02:03.030
Month three gives six hundred and three dollars

00:02:03.131 --> 00:02:03.812
and one cent.

00:02:04.496 --> 00:02:06.791
It's one rule, used again and again.

00:02:07.676 --> 00:02:10.827
The next balance is one plus r, times

00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:12.353
this balance, plus M.

00:02:13.117 --> 00:02:15.045
The first deposit earns nothing in its own

00:02:15.086 --> 00:02:17.515
month, because it arrives at the end, after

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:17.998
the interest.

00:02:18.669 --> 00:02:21.342
A cash-flow diagram shows the same thing on

00:02:21.363 --> 00:02:22.006
a time line.

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:25.056
Time zero is when the account opens, and

00:02:25.197 --> 00:02:26.059
nothing goes in yet.

00:02:26.822 --> 00:02:29.367
Each arrow is one deposit, at the end

00:02:29.388 --> 00:02:29.968
of a period:

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:33.515
at one, two, three, and so on, up

00:02:33.536 --> 00:02:33.916
to n.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.054
So n periods give n deposits.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:39.626
The future value is the balance at the

00:02:39.828 --> 00:02:41.072
end, at time n,

00:02:41.595 --> 00:02:43.141
straight after the last deposit.

00:02:43.804 --> 00:02:45.836
That last deposit has had no time to

00:02:45.938 --> 00:02:47.003
earn any interest yet.

00:02:47.769 --> 00:02:48.312
Now the fund.

00:02:49.057 --> 00:02:51.381
A hundred thousand dollars earns four point eight

00:02:51.422 --> 00:02:53.905
per cent a year, compounded monthly, so the

00:02:53.946 --> 00:02:56.069
rate per month is zero point zero zero

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:56.349
four.

00:02:57.232 --> 00:02:59.105
In the first month it earns four hundred

00:02:59.147 --> 00:02:59.810
dollars interest.

00:03:00.335 --> 00:03:02.299
Then the withdrawal of one thousand and fifty

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:03.743
dollars ninety-one comes out,

00:03:04.246 --> 00:03:06.932
leaving ninety-nine thousand, three hundred and forty-nine dollars

00:03:07.054 --> 00:03:07.735
and nine cents.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:09.344
Here's the rule.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:13.076
The next balance is one plus r, times

00:03:13.137 --> 00:03:14.680
this balance, minus M.

00:03:15.285 --> 00:03:16.737
That's exactly the loan rule.

00:03:17.464 --> 00:03:19.616
A loan has the same cash flows, seen

00:03:19.637 --> 00:03:20.421
from the lender's side.

00:03:21.046 --> 00:03:23.282
One sum goes out, and equal amounts come

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:23.524
back.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:25.401
Why does the fund run down?

00:03:26.249 --> 00:03:27.994
Each month the withdrawal is bigger than the

00:03:28.076 --> 00:03:30.805
interest, so the balance drops, and next month

00:03:30.887 --> 00:03:32.070
there's less to earn interest on.

00:03:32.613 --> 00:03:34.860
So it falls slowly at first, then faster,

00:03:35.302 --> 00:03:37.148
and runs out at the hundred and twentieth

00:03:37.189 --> 00:03:38.693
withdrawal, which is ten years.

00:03:39.476 --> 00:03:41.441
That last one is a little smaller, because

00:03:41.462 --> 00:03:43.166
the withdrawal is rounded up to the cent.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.755
If you only took out four hundred dollars,

00:03:46.098 --> 00:03:48.486
exactly the interest, the balance would stay at

00:03:48.527 --> 00:03:49.771
a hundred thousand forever.

00:03:50.374 --> 00:03:52.317
The withdrawal was set, the same way a

00:03:52.438 --> 00:03:54.582
loan repayment is set, so that the fund

00:03:54.723 --> 00:03:55.825
lasts ten years.

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:57.455
One more condition.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:00.748
In this course, interest compounds as often as

00:04:00.789 --> 00:04:01.631
the payments are made,

00:04:02.334 --> 00:04:04.180
so the rate and the count match the

00:04:04.201 --> 00:04:04.561
payments.

00:04:05.303 --> 00:04:07.003
Five hundred dollars a month for two years,

00:04:07.124 --> 00:04:09.205
at six per cent a year compounded monthly,

00:04:09.686 --> 00:04:11.926
has r equal to zero point zero zero

00:04:11.987 --> 00:04:14.708
five per month, and n equal to twenty-four

00:04:14.749 --> 00:04:15.709
months, not two.

00:04:16.511 --> 00:04:18.888
For quarterly payments, divide the annual rate by

00:04:18.989 --> 00:04:19.190
four.

00:04:19.675 --> 00:04:21.709
For fortnightly, divide by twenty-six.

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:24.822
In this course, every payment comes at the

00:04:24.963 --> 00:04:25.685
end of its period.

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:27.404
Here's a way to spot one.

00:04:27.951 --> 00:04:30.831
Same size, same gap, interest every gap.

00:04:31.475 --> 00:04:33.497
In goes a contribution, or out goes a

00:04:33.538 --> 00:04:36.261
withdrawal, but always the same amount, at the

00:04:36.302 --> 00:04:38.985
same interval, with interest compounding in between.

00:04:41.727 --> 00:04:42.156
Your turn.

00:04:42.563 --> 00:04:44.457
Which of these four are annuities?

00:04:45.243 --> 00:04:46.233
Pause here to answer.

00:04:57.176 --> 00:04:58.972
B and D are annuities.

00:04:59.517 --> 00:05:02.445
B has equal deposits every fortnight, with interest

00:05:02.486 --> 00:05:03.046
each fortnight.

00:05:03.769 --> 00:05:06.311
D starts with one sum and makes equal

00:05:06.332 --> 00:05:08.553
yearly withdrawals, with interest each year.

00:05:09.276 --> 00:05:11.449
C isn't, because the deposits aren't equal.

00:05:12.557 --> 00:05:14.226
Choosing A is the classic mistake.

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:17.444
It earns compound interest, but compound interest alone

00:05:17.646 --> 00:05:18.430
isn't an annuity.

00:05:18.693 --> 00:05:19.806
There are no regular payments.

00:05:21.645 --> 00:05:22.013
Last one.

00:05:22.983 --> 00:05:25.570
Write a recurrence for this account, and find

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.354
its balance after three deposits.

00:05:28.076 --> 00:05:29.039
Pause here to answer.

00:05:39.808 --> 00:05:41.753
Six per cent a year is zero point

00:05:41.814 --> 00:05:43.358
zero zero five a month.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:46.602
So A sub n plus one equals one

00:05:46.683 --> 00:05:49.144
point zero zero five A sub n, plus

00:05:49.205 --> 00:05:51.727
four hundred, with A nought equal to zero.

00:05:52.369 --> 00:05:54.915
Then A three is one thousand two hundred

00:05:54.936 --> 00:05:56.439
and six dollars and one cent.

00:05:57.244 --> 00:05:58.959
A one isn't four hundred and two.

00:05:59.364 --> 00:06:01.439
The first deposit earns no interest in its

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:01.943
first month.

00:06:03.648 --> 00:06:06.878
So an annuity is equal payments at regular

00:06:06.919 --> 00:06:10.188
times, in or out, with interest compounding every

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:10.610
period.

00:06:11.274 --> 00:06:12.318
Super works this way too.

00:06:13.184 --> 00:06:15.285
Picture a super account where two hundred dollars

00:06:15.326 --> 00:06:16.986
goes in at the end of every month,

00:06:17.427 --> 00:06:19.108
earning a steady six per cent a year.

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:22.997
After five years, that's sixty deposits, twelve thousand

00:06:23.018 --> 00:06:23.879
dollars in total.

00:06:24.882 --> 00:06:27.604
Will the balance be exactly twelve thousand, a

00:06:27.645 --> 00:06:28.124
little more,

00:06:28.646 --> 00:06:29.525
or a lot more?

00:06:32.488 --> 00:06:33.371
Here are the key notes.

00:06:33.894 --> 00:06:35.662
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:35.683 --> 00:06:36.164
to copy them.
